# Commit footer after changes

Every **new git commit** automatically updates `CURRENT_GIT_COMMIT.txt` at the project root (via `post-commit` hook). The user can open that file anytime for the latest restore line.

When you **edit project source** or **create a commit** in a turn, end your user-facing reply with:

```
---
Git: `<hash>` — `<commit subject>`
```

Read the hash from:

```powershell
git log -1 --format="%h %s"
```

Or from `CURRENT_GIT_COMMIT.txt` → line `FOOTER=...` after a commit was made.

If the turn included edits but **no commit yet**, add `(uncommitted)`:

```
---
Git: `7a4f2dd` — Add auto-checkpoint hooks… (uncommitted)
```

If you **created a commit** in the turn, show the **new** hash without `(uncommitted)`.

If the turn was **read-only** (questions only, no file edits), omit the footer.

Keep the footer to **one line** — the user copies it back into chat to restore: `Γύρισε στο <hash>`.
